WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(audience applauding)
- [Kelly] How are you?

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:03.160
- Look at you in New York.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.550
- You look incredible, you're glowing.

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:07.710
Do you love New York,
do you love being here?

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:10.710
- I'm only really ever
here when I do a show.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.140
So I'll live here for months on end.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:13.973
- Yeah, that's living here though.

00:00:13.973 --> 00:00:15.900
- But it's only sort of every 10 years

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.160
that I end up on Broadway.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.530
- But when I'm here, I love it.

00:00:19.530 --> 00:00:20.850
- Yeah, did you grow up doing theater?

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:21.840
Is that why you love it so much?

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:22.673
- Yeah.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:00:22.673 --> 00:00:26.280
- Yeah, I think I was 28 before
I had a television audition.

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:29.190
It was all theater through
my teens and my twenties.

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:30.840
- I love hearing that though, for actors

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.510
that are still like pushing
and they're like, man, I'm 28.

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:34.800
Like, I haven't hit how I want to yet.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.440
Like I love hearing that,
and you're this huge TV star.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.480
You just never know like, timing.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.400
- And back then sort of, I was hitting,

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.790
I was just getting into
television in my about to be 30

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:46.410
and it was suddenly too old.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:47.400
- Yeah.
- I was playing dads

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.440
in my first-
- Yeah, so it's awesome.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.120
I heard that you got to do
this and I've never seen

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.010
Elton John live and I'm so jealous.

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:53.930
But you got to go to his-

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:54.763
- You haven't?
- [Kelly] I haven't.

00:00:54.763 --> 00:00:56.430
- That's crazy.
- I was poor growing up, man.

00:00:56.430 --> 00:00:58.830
And then by the time I
made money, I'm so busy.

00:00:58.830 --> 00:01:00.450
I only catch shows like when I can.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:01.530
But Elton is incredible.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:02.940
And I heard that you
were at his final concert

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:03.773
here in North America.

00:01:03.773 --> 00:01:05.760
- Well, so one of my,
a story I love to tell

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.350
when I was doing theater in high school,

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:09.660
I was doing a production
of Godspell in 11th grade.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.670
And one of my co-stars was David Furnish,

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.920
who is now Elton John's husband.

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.800
So we've had a relationship
through our lives

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.100
and I've had one with Elton
that see, I don't know,

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.320
10, 12 times I've seen
Elton over the years.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:23.520
- [Kelly] Oh my God, you lucky man.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:24.600
- I know, it's so great.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:26.700
It's also allowed for
some crazy friendships,

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.185
like the people I got to meet
through that period of time.

00:01:30.185 --> 00:01:33.480
'Cause you know, a lot of
actors look up to other actors.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.660
I was just in awe of musicians always.

00:01:36.660 --> 00:01:38.940
I, when I was 15, I just wanted to be you.

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:40.200
I wanted to be a rock star.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.033
I wanted to do what you do.
- That's so funny

00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:44.610
'cause my favorites are
actors, that sing, but yeah.

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:46.701
- We're shallow and empty.
- [Kelly] No!

00:01:46.701 --> 00:01:47.880
- I wouldn't worry about it.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.200
But so I wanted, so I loved
Elton and I got to meet

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.960
Bernie Taupin who writes
all of Elton's lyrics

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:56.880
and we became friends through the years.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:57.713
- Great writer.

00:01:57.713 --> 00:02:00.990
- And I wanted to show
this, so I was invited.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:01.830
- [Kelly] You recreated it.

00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:06.830
- Yeah yeah, so this is Bernie
Taupin's 70th birthday party.

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:10.350
There's Bernie, Elton,
Alice Cooper, and me.

00:02:10.350 --> 00:02:13.200
And I based this photograph
on this photograph,

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.450
which is from 1972, I think.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:16.890
Alice Cooper,
- [Kelly] You needed to undo

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:18.120
your shirt a little bit more.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:19.649
Give some more chest hair.

00:02:19.649 --> 00:02:21.120
- Do you recognize who that is?

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.700
Between Alice and Elton is Paul Lynde

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.680
from the "Hollywood Squares".

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:28.980
So I'm Paul Lynde in
2000, whatever that was

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.810
- Next time just gold
chain, more chest hair.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:32.630
- Gold chain, yeah.
- Yes, yeah.

00:02:32.630 --> 00:02:34.710
- So I mean, when I'm 15 I'm in my bedroom

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.390
pretending to be Alice Cooper.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:39.090
Like, that was how I became an actor

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:40.530
was wanting to be a rock star.

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:42.300
- Isn't it funny though,
I find that the people,

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.280
'cause I met him years ago, Alice.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:47.400
And he was so nice, like so sweet.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:50.070
And he is this huge rock
star with goth makeup

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:50.903
and all this stuff.

00:02:50.903 --> 00:02:52.590
I was like, I was kind
of afraid to meet him,

00:02:52.590 --> 00:02:53.490
but he's so nice.

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:55.676
- He's so nice, and it's all a character.

00:02:55.676 --> 00:02:58.080
It's a character that I still
do at Halloween occasionally.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.570
- Yeah, well, it's a
great one, it's a classic.

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:02.520
So what did you listen to music as a kid?

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:03.353
You listened to Alice?

00:03:03.353 --> 00:03:04.950
- It was that, Queen was my big one.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.750
- [Kelly] Oh, Freddie Mercury.

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:09.120
- And my father loved
when I'd put on, you know,

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.641
tights in my bedroom and

00:03:11.641 --> 00:03:14.880
(audience laughing)

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.710
I'd go downstairs to get
something from the kitchen.

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:18.480
He was like, "You stay in
your room in those things!"

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:20.040
- [Kelly] Oh my God.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:23.220
- But yeah, that's who I
wanted to be very much.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:24.480
- By the way, you are my son,

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.820
My seven year old son is
like, he looks like a little,

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:29.160
it's like Harry Styles
is that generation now.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:32.550
'Cause he, his wardrobe is so
eclectic and I love it that

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.390
Harry is like a
representative for him because

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:37.800
he is also talented, but
he also dresses so fun

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:39.210
and eccentric, you know, a bit.

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:41.730
And my son my feels
comfortable and he's seven

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:43.530
and that's so awesome because, you know,

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:45.600
sometimes they're always
like, boys can't do that.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:46.433
And I always look at him, I'm like,

00:03:46.433 --> 00:03:48.330
boys can do anything they want, you know?

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:51.735
- And my my son's 21 in
college and this is the time

00:03:51.735 --> 00:03:54.840
to be in college is just
the experimentation.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:55.950
- Yeah, you never know.

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:57.480
Well, what was your first concert?

00:03:57.480 --> 00:03:58.313
Very first.

00:03:58.313 --> 00:04:00.240
- I'd love to say that it was Queen,

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:02.520
but that was really my second in 78.

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:07.353
My first was ELO and
Meatloaf opened for them.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:10.060
- I actually loved Meatloaf.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:11.734
- I know.
- Yes.

00:04:11.734 --> 00:04:13.530
♪ I would do anything ♪

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:16.260
I loved it, it was very operatic, yeah.

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.660
Well that first album wasn't even out yet.

00:04:18.660 --> 00:04:20.070
That's "Paradise by the Dashboard Light"

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:21.720
and all that stuff hadn't really dropped,

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:23.310
which is why they were an opening band,

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:26.220
but they were an opening band
in front of 70,000 people.

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:27.660
- Oh my gosh.
- And there's, you know,

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:30.060
he's little, he's that big on the stage,

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:31.440
but that album, wow.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.110
- Oh my gosh, that's
a great first concert.

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:35.130
I can't, I can't even remember.

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:37.320
Mine was either Aerosmith or Celine Dion,

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:38.570
which are very different.

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:41.730
Very passionate, both, but yeah,

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:44.460
- I saw Aerosmith too in like
70, I think it was 78, 79.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:46.200
- Oh man, that changed my,

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:47.610
we were gonna get married, yeah.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:49.443
- You and Steven?
- [Kelly] Yeah, I was, yeah.

00:04:49.443 --> 00:04:50.276
- Was he aware of it?

00:04:50.276 --> 00:04:52.860
- No, but he would love me.
- Of course he would.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:55.500
- Yeah, no, I went to the
concert and I just love

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:57.000
that feeling that you get inspired people.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.350
That's how I get by actors by the way

00:04:58.350 --> 00:05:00.060
because it's such a
different world for me.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:01.620
- Right.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:03.390
- But I get that with
certain musicians as well.

00:05:03.390 --> 00:05:05.280
And just seeing him, you
know, (mimicking Steven Tyler)

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.110
with the fan and everything.

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:09.775
I just, yeah, I would
pretend I was Steven Tyler.

00:05:09.775 --> 00:05:10.873
- That whole (indistinct)
thing that he used to,

00:05:10.873 --> 00:05:13.615
I mean, he can still do it, but wow.

00:05:13.615 --> 00:05:16.530
What he was doing in '73 was nuts.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:18.090
- We did a photo shoot
though later in life.

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:19.320
And have you ever met
like one of your heroes?

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:22.107
And it was like, he was so
like, passionate that he was,

00:05:22.107 --> 00:05:25.440
and my dream was for him
to go yaka yaka on my face,

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:26.943
but then when he was actually doing it,

00:05:26.943 --> 00:05:28.693
I was like, ha ha ha.

00:05:30.069 --> 00:05:31.650
I was like that's so funny, so funny.

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:32.850
And I just got so nervous.

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.790
I was overwhelmed with
passion deep inside.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:36.780
So you're back on Broadway.

00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:38.070
This is called "The Cottage", right?

00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:42.180
- The Cottage, which is your
classic English sex farce.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:43.563
- [Kelly] Okay, I'm in.

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.350
- It's hysterical, although it is,

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:47.350
oh there I am there.

00:05:48.209 --> 00:05:49.748
(audience applauding)

00:05:49.748 --> 00:05:51.360
- With that mustache.

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.580
- It's actually, it's
a new play written by

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:54.660
a young American woman.

00:05:54.660 --> 00:05:56.430
But she just wanted to
write in the classic sort

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:57.750
of Noel Coward style.

00:05:57.750 --> 00:05:59.325
So it's very, it's old fashioned,

00:05:59.325 --> 00:06:01.440
but it's more from the female perspective.

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.600
It's like a feminist Noel Coward play.

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:05.373
But it's dirty as hell and-

00:06:06.432 --> 00:06:07.290
- I would love it.

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:08.730
- You would love it, come see it.

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:09.720
- That's amazing.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:11.130
Are you working with Laura Bell Bundy?

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:11.994
- [Eric] Yes.
- In it?

00:06:11.994 --> 00:06:13.440
Yeah, yeah, yeah, I've run
into her a couple times.

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:17.220
- Laura Bell, she was
"Legally Blonde" the musical,

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.070
She was Tony nominated
and we are such a team

00:06:20.070 --> 00:06:22.560
at the top of that show, I just
couldn't do it without her.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.660
- It's so funny, now all I'm
seeing is Freddie Mercury

00:06:24.660 --> 00:06:26.940
when I look at you right now.

00:06:26.940 --> 00:06:28.170
You were literally talking and I was like,

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:29.340
listen, listen, listen.

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:30.600
And I can't, I was like, I can't,

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:31.920
like, now I'm just
picturing you in a movie

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:33.030
playing Freddie Mercury.

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:33.960
So I have a question.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.540
So whenever an actor directs
you, like Jason Alexander

00:06:36.540 --> 00:06:38.820
who directs this, is that different than

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:39.990
when it's not an actor?

00:06:39.990 --> 00:06:41.340
Do they direct in a different way?

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:42.930
- Most actors love other actors

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:44.400
directing them for the most part.

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:47.040
I particularly love it 'cause
Jason and I are old friends,

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:48.960
not from NBC or whatever,

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:51.217
but from being dads at the same school.

00:06:51.217 --> 00:06:52.410
- Oh, you have a shorthand.

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:55.087
- Yeah, yeah, and he directed
me in a production of

00:06:55.087 --> 00:06:57.840
"The Fantastics" 11 years ago.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:06:59.580
So we had a great shorthand.

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:04.200
And as a comedy legend,
I mean, I was just so,

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:07.020
it's so great, this is a
really freaking funny comedy.

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:09.030
- It's gotta be good too
to work with your friends.

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:11.519
Like that's what I am at the
age now to where I'm just like,

00:07:11.519 --> 00:07:13.710
I just wanna work with
people I love to work with

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:15.270
and I just wanna do it, you know?

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:17.760
- At the same time I've always
loved meeting new people,

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:18.840
having new cast.

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:20.550
And I just gotta talk about
the cast of "The Cottage"

00:07:20.550 --> 00:07:21.383
for one second.

00:07:21.383 --> 00:07:24.090
They are all Broadway
pros, they are amazing.

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:26.040
And particularly my friend Dana Steingold,

00:07:26.040 --> 00:07:28.054
who is a huge Kelly Clarkson fan.

00:07:28.054 --> 00:07:28.887
- She has great taste.

00:07:28.887 --> 00:07:30.630
- And I just said her name.

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:32.040
I just said it on television.

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:32.910
There you go, Dana, that's for you.

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:34.950
- So what is your favorite,
do you like acting

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:37.952
on stage like that, or
do you like acting on TV?

00:07:37.952 --> 00:07:39.930
'Cause everybody always
asks me to act and I go,

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:42.360
I hate acting on TV or movie
anything like that I hate

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:45.870
because I don't like, I
like the constant stream of,

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:47.580
'cause I grew up in musical theater.

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:49.200
I like the stage acting better.

00:07:49.200 --> 00:07:50.033
So what do you-

00:07:50.033 --> 00:07:52.290
- Yeah, I mean, if I did it all the time,

00:07:52.290 --> 00:07:54.240
perhaps I'd yearn for a little of it.

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:56.027
But I love being on stage.

00:07:56.027 --> 00:07:58.070
I miss it and I'm hoping
to do a little bit more.

00:07:58.070 --> 00:07:59.010
- Breaking up the monotony is cool though.

00:07:59.010 --> 00:08:01.800
- Yeah, what was your musical
that you did in high school?

00:08:01.800 --> 00:08:03.960
- I did a View, but the
one that I felt like

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:07.230
I finally was like, oh man,
I could do this for a living,

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:09.510
was I was "Seven Brides, Seven Brothers".

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:10.950
- Oh wow, okay.
- I was the lead in that.

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:13.680
So I was like, and it was double cast.

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:14.513
And so that-

00:08:14.513 --> 00:08:16.160
- That's a lot of brides
and a lot of brothers.

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:19.380
- Yeah, yeah, well, there's a
political backstory to that,

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:20.580
that I'll save for off camera.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:24.270
But no, and we only got one
show and we learned all of it.

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:26.730
So I was like, yeah,
the other cast got more.

00:08:26.730 --> 00:08:28.950
But anyway, but it was
like incredible though

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:29.970
to that moment.

00:08:29.970 --> 00:08:31.140
Do you remember that
moment you're on stage?

00:08:31.140 --> 00:08:32.640
It was that I was singing
"One Man" in that,

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:37.050
and I just felt this
like overwhelming purpose

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:38.430
and it was magical.

00:08:38.430 --> 00:08:39.263
- A hundred percent.

00:08:39.263 --> 00:08:40.800
For me, that production of
"Godspell" I was talking about.

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:42.930
It wasn't even the evening performances

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:43.770
that we eventually did,

00:08:43.770 --> 00:08:46.500
it was that assembly where
they bring the school together

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:48.313
and they do a thing for the
football team, whatever.

00:08:48.313 --> 00:08:49.146
- We did that too.

00:08:49.146 --> 00:08:51.900
- And now a theater class
is gonna do a song from

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:53.340
their new show "Godspell".

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:56.190
And it was that, it was being Jesus

00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:57.203
in the rainbow suspenders
- Amongst your peers.

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:00.720
- Peers who had never much cared for me.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:01.800
- Worship me!

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:04.494
But suddenly now it was
like, oh, okay, we get it.

00:09:04.494 --> 00:09:05.730
And I was like, this is it.

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:07.530
- Wait, so speaking of
friends coming to a show,

00:09:07.530 --> 00:09:09.180
I always tell people to never tell me

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:10.013
if someone comes to a show.

00:09:10.013 --> 00:09:11.790
- [Eric] Oh really?
- Yeah, I hate knowing.

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:12.623
Do you like knowing?

00:09:12.623 --> 00:09:14.880
- I like know, particularly
in a long run like this,

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:16.440
where you're doing the same show,

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:18.458
we've done it 110 times now.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:09:18.458 --> 00:09:22.380
- It helps me to see it through new eyes.

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:23.213
- Oh okay.
- I can get excited

00:09:23.213 --> 00:09:24.570
because I know, so-and-so's out there,

00:09:24.570 --> 00:09:28.020
and they're English, so
they'll hate my accent.

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:28.860
Or they're
- They're judgy.

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:31.560
- Or they're really judgy so
they'll hate the whole show.

00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:33.090
- Oh man, I get, that makes me nerv,

00:09:33.090 --> 00:09:34.260
I don't get nervous on stage,

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:36.330
but when I know that friends
or family or something,

00:09:36.330 --> 00:09:38.010
I'm like, I don't wanna know, yeah.

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:40.170
- I don't know why it
gives me a little kick.

00:09:40.170 --> 00:09:41.460
- So one time speaking of that though,

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:43.170
you actually surprised Sean Hayes, right?

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:44.010
Was it in Chicago?

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:46.590
- Yeah, so Sean, I dunno
if you got a chance

00:09:46.590 --> 00:09:48.903
to see "Goodnight Oscar",
but he won the Tony for it.

00:09:48.903 --> 00:09:49.736
- [Kelly] He's so talented.

00:09:49.736 --> 00:09:52.290
- He's so incredible.
- [Kelly] and sweet.

00:09:52.290 --> 00:09:57.290
- There's us together, but
he did it in Chicago first

00:09:57.360 --> 00:09:59.340
and I knew he was doing
it, it was very low key.

00:09:59.340 --> 00:10:00.870
This is like a year and a half ago.

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:03.330
But I flew in to see him and surprise him.

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:06.090
And I told him that night,
you're going to New York, right?

00:10:06.090 --> 00:10:07.147
He said, yeah, hopefully, and I said,

00:10:07.147 --> 00:10:08.940
"Yeah, you're gonna win the Tony."

00:10:08.940 --> 00:10:12.410
- Oh my God, you're like,
Nostradamus, you predicted that.

00:10:12.410 --> 00:10:13.573
- It was pretty clear,
he was pretty awesome.

00:10:13.573 --> 00:10:15.030
- Yeah, he's so talented.

00:10:15.030 --> 00:10:19.293
So have you ever had
huge mistakes on stage?

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:21.990
Because it's live, you can't stop.

00:10:21.990 --> 00:10:25.050
- This show a lot, we have a
lot of prop things go wrong,

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:27.630
which leads to a lot of improv and funny.

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:29.250
But I haven't had terrible mistake.

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:30.180
The the one I always tell just

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:32.820
'cause it was the beginning of my career.

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:34.710
I had a sort of a standing over there

00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:36.690
with a spear part in a
production of "King Lear"

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:39.150
giant production in Canada
at the Stratford Festival.

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:42.210
And one night I was just,
the fire in the center

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:43.860
of the stage and Lear is talking

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:45.690
and I know something bad's gonna happen.

00:10:45.690 --> 00:10:47.730
I feel sick, I gotta go.

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:49.620
But I don't, I keep
waiting and I keep waiting.

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:51.690
It's a long scene, and
finally I turned to go

00:10:51.690 --> 00:10:54.780
and I fainted dead away
and my helmet bounced

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:57.270
into the audience and my spear fell down.

00:10:57.270 --> 00:11:02.085
Lear apparently went
(grunts) "Bear him, hence".

00:11:02.085 --> 00:11:03.480
(audience laughing)

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:05.850
And so somebody picked me
up and then took me off

00:11:05.850 --> 00:11:09.150
and I sort of came to at the stage door

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:10.440
and he hovered over me.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:12.367
He'd finished the scene and
he came and he finished.

00:11:12.367 --> 00:11:14.021
"Are you all right there boy?"

00:11:14.021 --> 00:11:16.181
- Oh my God, he unplugged the whole thing.

00:11:16.181 --> 00:11:17.124
That's amazing.

00:11:17.124 --> 00:11:21.180
- Yes, and he went (grunts).

00:11:21.180 --> 00:11:23.100
So note to self, don't faint.

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:25.080
- But that's what I love about stage.

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:26.820
That's what is, every night is different.

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:28.890
It could be the same play, same musical,

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:30.810
but these things happen and I love that.

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:32.730
The energy of that, I think
that's pretty magical.

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:33.810
- We've had a thing we do in this show

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:35.640
that I came up with in
rehearsal where I'm trying to

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:39.420
get rid of a damning brassiere
that is gonna give me away

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:41.643
and I throw it into the audience and-

00:11:41.643 --> 00:11:42.990
- Like a rockstar.

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:44.430
You are Freddie Mercury.

00:11:44.430 --> 00:11:46.380
- And then whatever I do with the person

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:47.640
that catches it is fun.

00:11:47.640 --> 00:11:50.280
But once in a while the person
has thrown it back on stage,

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:54.300
which I love 'cause then
you can make stuff out.

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:55.320
- Yeah.
- It's pretty fun.

00:11:55.320 --> 00:11:56.560
- Yeah, and then you're
like a comedian on stage.

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:57.870
- Yeah.
- I love that.

00:11:57.870 --> 00:11:59.940
Well, I loved the side eye.

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:01.140
Well, let's take another break.

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:03.330
You can see Eric in "The
Cottage" on Broadway

00:12:03.330 --> 00:12:06.030
at the Hayes Theater now
through October 29th.

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:09.270
And his podcast, "Just Jack
and Will" with Sean Hayes

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:11.640
and Eric McCormick is
available wherever you get your

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:12.990
podcasts or like to listen.

